{0 Email}

{!Workero.email} registers the handler for
{{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/email-routing/email-workers/}Email Workers}.
Cloudflare Email Routing calls it for each message sent to an address that you
route to the Worker. The handler gets an {!Workero.Email.t}, the [env] and the
[ctx], and returns [unit].

{1 Configure}

Turn on Email Routing for the domain. In the dashboard, add a custom address
and set its action to "Send to a Worker". To forward a message, add the
destination address to Email Routing first and verify it. [wrangler.toml] needs
no email setting for the handler.

{1 Handle a message}

The handler must decide what happens to the message. It does one of three
things:

- {!Workero.Email.forward} sends the message to a verified address.
- {!Workero.Email.reply} sends a reply to the sender.
- {!Workero.Email.set_reject} refuses the message with a permanent SMTP error.

{@ocaml[
let () =
  Workero.email (fun message _env _ctx ->
      let open Workero in
      match Email.header message "subject" with
      | Some subject when String.length subject > 0 ->
          Email.forward
            ~headers:[ ("X-Routed-By", "workero") ]
            message "me@example.org"
      | _ -> Email.set_reject message "a subject is required")
]}

{!Workero.Email.from} is the envelope sender and {!Workero.Email.to_} is the
envelope recipient. They can differ from the [From] and [To] headers. Read the
headers with {!Workero.Email.header}, {!Workero.Email.header_all} and
{!Workero.Email.headers}. {!Workero.Email.raw_size} is the size of the raw
message in bytes.

{!Workero.Email.forward} takes extra headers. The runtime accepts only names
that start with [X-]. It raises {!Workero.Js_error} when the address is not a
verified destination.

{1 Read the raw message}

{!Workero.Email.raw_text} and {!Workero.Email.raw_bytes} read the whole message,
with its headers, and suspend like {!Workero.Promise.await}. The raw message
can be read once. A second read raises {!Workero.Js_error}.

{@ocaml[
let () =
  Workero.email (fun message _env _ctx ->
      let open Workero in
      if Email.raw_size message > 1_000_000 then
        Email.set_reject message "message too large"
      else
        let raw = Email.raw_text message in
        Console.log raw;
        Email.forward message "me@example.org")
]}

The program holds the whole message in memory, and the bytes are copied once
from JavaScript into the memory of the wasm program. For a large message, read
{!Workero.Email.raw} as a {!Workero.Stream.t} and handle it in chunks with
{!Workero.Stream.iter}. Check {!Workero.Email.raw_size} before you read.

{1 Reply}

{!Workero.Email.reply} sends a complete message, with headers, as the reply. The
runtime accepts a reply only when the sender allows it, so the raw message needs
a [Message-ID] header and an [In-Reply-To] header with the [Message-ID] of the
incoming message. [~from] must be an address of the Worker's domain, and [~to_]
the sender:

{@ocaml[
let () =
  Workero.email (fun message _env _ctx ->
      let open Workero in
      let id = Option.value (Email.header message "message-id") ~default:"" in
      Email.reply message ~from:"support@example.com" ~to_:(Email.from message)
        (String.concat "\r\n"
           [
             "From: support@example.com";
             "To: " ^ Email.from message;
             "Message-ID: <reply-1@example.com>";
             "In-Reply-To: " ^ id;
             "Subject: Received";
             "";
             "We got your message.";
             "";
           ]))
]}

{!Workero.Email.reply_stream} takes the same complete message as a
{!Workero.Stream.t}, for example from {!Workero.Stream.pipe}. Write it from a
{!Workero.Context.wait_until} function, because the runtime reads the stream
while the call waits.

[reply] creates the [EmailMessage] from the [cloudflare:email] module when it is
called. [workerd] does not provide that module. See
{{!page-known_issues}Support and known issues}.

{1 Errors}

An exception that escapes the handler rejects the event Promise, and Cloudflare
treats the delivery as failed. Catch {!Workero.Js_error} from {!Workero.Email.forward} and
{!Workero.Email.reply} to answer with {!Workero.Email.set_reject} instead.

{1 Testing}

[workerd] 2026-10-19 cannot deliver an email. A service binding has [fetch],
[scheduled] and [queue] methods but no [email] method, and the runtime has no
[cloudflare:email] module. To
test a handler, import the generated [index.js] in a small test worker, build a
message object with the fields of [ForwardableEmailMessage] ([from], [to],
[headers], [raw], [rawSize], [setReject], [forward], [reply]) and call
[handlers.email(message, env, ctx)]. [test/worker/services/email-harness.mjs]
does this, and [test/worker/cases/email.mjs] has the cases. The test worker gives
a stand-in [cloudflare:email] module for [reply]. The tests do not prove that
the real runtime accepts the messages.
